you  right now for morning rounds our look at  the medical news of the week for the  millions of Americans suffering from  dementia unpaid caregivers such as  family and friends often produce  invaluable assistance a poll out this  week from the University of Michigan  asked these caregivers about their  personal experiences 148 individuals  took part in the survey all of them were  between the ages of 50 and 80 62% were  women and nearly half had another job in  addition to caregiving here to break  down the findings and more is CBS News  contributor dr. Tara Narula  great to see it okay to see you so this  is kind of an unusual study you don't  see a lot of studies about caregivers  what did they find we don't see a lot of  research about it we don't talk about it  enough but this is really a group of  unsung heroes they don't get paid they  don't get a day off they don't get  recognized and many times they're caring  for people who don't recognize them in  this survey 60% of the time it was for  parent 19% of the time was for a spouse  we're not talking about weeks or days  this was for the most part years of  caregiving forty percent of the time for  nine hours or more a day is equivalent  to full-time employment so what was  interesting was that well 78 percent  said that it was stressful eighty-five  percent did in fact find it rewarding  yeah and 91 percent said it gave them  perspective meaning it gave them time to  think about how would they want to have  their caregiving needs met when they  became older so what are their some of  the challenges these caregivers faces  with the pole they're big challenges as  you mentioned this is a group that's  essentially over 50 many have their own  kids many are working 14 percent said  that they did not feel they had the  physical and mental health capable of  taking or the abilities to take care of  someone else 60 percent essentially said  that it took away from their abilities  to deal with their own daily  responsibilities to go to the doctor to  have time with family and friends so  lots of challenges for this group so how  do we help caregivers well we need to  provide them access to resources and  really look at this as a team effort so  we need to do a better job at promoting  social service agencies classes family  therapy essentially anything that can  help support them healthcare providers  need to play a big role too in screening  and asking about caregiving  responsibilities and family and friends  really need to pitch in you know I  recently had a patient in my office for  cardiac um  and when I started talking to her she  broke down in tears and told me she  essentially had started getting all of  these issues a couple years ago when she  gave up her life moved home from Europe  and had to care for her father we spent  the rest of the visit really talking  about how to get her in to see a  therapist recognition and  acknowledgement that these people like  these folks exist and they are dealing  with a lot of duress that's right having  it seems like the first step okay our  next topic the beating of our hearts a  recent study in the journal Circulation  looked at one of the most common types  of irregular heartbeat atrial  fibrillation researchers examine more  than 79,000 patient records from four  studies out of Europe none of the  patients had been diagnosed with the  condition at the start they had a median  age of 49 and were followed for an  average of about 12 years Terry you're  our resident cardiologist what exactly  is atrial fibrillation so this is the  most common arrhythmia essentially the  upper chambers of the heart are supposed  to be synchronously to beat and pump the  blood down to the lower chambers in afib  essentially what happens is those upper  chambers beat irregularly they quiver so  there is not efficient movement of blood  down to those lower chambers which means  it can stagnate in the upper chambers  form clots and those clots can go to the  brain this is why it is puts people at  increased risk of stroke essentially  four to five times increased risk there  are things that are associated that can  increase the risk of developing afib for  instance aging hypertensive heart  disease or if you've had a history of  heart attack heart failure valvular  heart disease obesity alcohol  medications thyroid problems lots of  things that can predispose some people  can be symptomatic they can feel  palpitations shortness of breath chest  pain other people can have no symptoms  and it can be something where it comes  and goes or something that's chronic and  what did this study find and what were  the major findings well the major issue  is that the lifetime prevalence is  really profound it's you know  essentially in this study about 24%  developed a fib by the age of 90 the  incidence increases for men by the time  they're 50 and older for women it's a  decade later 60 and older the incidence  starts to go up it increases mortality  to a great degree so 3 and a half times  increased more Towell in this study and  one of the biggest things that put  people at increased risk was increased  BMI or body mass index  overweight or obese the nice thing about  knowing that is that it is something  that's modifiable so people can work on  getting healthy eating a healthy diet  losing weight and exercising okay  finally there is no crystal ball in  medicine but using technology might help  predict which strains of the flu will  threaten us a new study in the journal  Science Translational Medicine showcases  a proposed flu forecasting model that  incorporates ongoing evolutionary  changes in the virus using past data the  model was able to predict outbreak  levels of a certain strain of the flu  accurately from 2002 to 2016 the hope is  this type of model will help flu  fighting strategies worldwide in the  future yeah really important because  usually we just look at how fast the  virus is traveling and we can't really  assess that until the season starts but  with this you're looking at how it's  mutating and changing and that will give  you an earlier sense the ability to  forecast a little bit earlier it's very  heartening to know that the tech has  helped in recent years in terms of  targeting the right strain that's right  alright get your flu vaccine yeah  everybody get their flu I actually got  my nerve doc dr. Tara Narula great to  see you

